1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,280
A few people asked me what you're actually meant to do with Scout when your team's on defence, or getting completely rolled, or you're playing on some narrow map like Dust Bowl, where there's barely anywhere to move.

2
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:12,000
And they all sound like different questions, but it's pretty much the same problem.

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:18,440
You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore, because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well.

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:25,180
Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated, you've got space to run around, and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up.

5
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,300
But the second your team loses all that pressure and every doorway starts getting watched,

6
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game.

7
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,560
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit, using chip damage,

8
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:46,900
sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on, and waiting for someone to

9
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:50,740
overextend instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned.

10
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:55,420
Because even when an opening looks really good, you still have to think about whether your team is alive,

11
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:59,480
whether anyone can actually help you, and whether dying for that play is even worth it,

12
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:05,500
especially when you're defending on red in payload or attack defend, because you can't take your life for granted like Blue Team Cat,

13
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:14,140
because those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous, and one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

14
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:18,080
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows.

15
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:27,360
You can make half the enemy team turn around, pull someone away from the objective, or force their medic backwards, but your team still has to notice and do something with it.

16
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:41,580
So we're going to go through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm going to lightly commentate, and how to stay useful when you can't hold W, how to know when to commit, and how to tell when you made a bad play versus when your team just didn't take advantage of what you created.

17
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:46,440
In downtime situations like this on defence, chip damage is sometimes all you can really do,

18
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:50,340
but it's more important than people think. It makes people look back, slows them down,

19
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:54,820
and forces them to look for heals. And make sure not just when pestering people, but in general,

20
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:59,020
make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any position with Scout. It's crucial

21
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:03,060
that if you get the chance, to not let engineers get their sentry built in the first place. If it's

22
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:07,420
level 3, you alert your team, and when your combo comes out to deal with it, help them if you can

23
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:13,360
At that very moment, but otherwise pay attention to the flanks. The engineer was on the ball here, and he starts building again

24
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,480
So I call it out to my soldier friend, and he deals with it straight away

25
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:24,100
The engineer is probably thinking oh my god. I'm so unlucky they keep walking out soon as I build my stuff, but it isn't luck

26
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:25,520
I'm scouting out these areas

27
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:45,460
I being an actual scout and I communicating what I say to my team At the same time I not overextending I trading these two flanks like they my post I contesting them and I cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through But I not running forward trying to take a 1v2 I just taking it slow taking my time and making sure these areas stay contested because after all we playing defence

28
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:50,180
We're not the ones attacking. You don't need to hold W as much as you might think just because you're playing Scout.

29
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:07,660
Right here, I'm backed into a corner by a pyro and a scout.

30
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:11,500
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go, but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

31
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:15,740
I run up it, jump backwards, and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward

32
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:17,080
over the pyro's head.

33
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:20,940
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed, and just happens to set me up to kill

34
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:21,940
the scout as well.

35
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:25,660
That's one of the best skills you can have as a scout, not just seamlessly using anything

36
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:29,660
It's not looking around you, but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

37
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:37,660
It's a bit like Emmet becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except you're looking at every box, railing or tiny slope and thinking,

38
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:43,660
can I use it as high ground? Basically any type of elevated platform will let you get extra height and reposition with less risk.

39
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:51,660
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding a boxed in last with two obvious flanks.

40
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:55,660
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

41
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:59,660
Scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention without just throwing his life away.

42
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:07,660
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you and drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

43
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:23,820
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend, and the enemy has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

44
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:32,400
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it. There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially when you're on defence.

45
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:39,080
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them down, and keep running time off the clock.

46
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:44,720
But you need to be alive to do this. Where blue team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through,

47
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:46,880
red is more about survivability.

48
00:04:53,620 --> 00:04:58,640
The previous clip was a good example of what Scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area,

49
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:20,599
But when your team is established and actually occupying the space you defending your role changes Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focused in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med and clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with and safely apply chip damage that forces blue to retreat or delays their push

50
00:05:21,119 --> 00:05:27,079
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without overcommitting and throwing away the position your team has already established.

51
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:51,679
Another question I got was how you're meant to play scout and narrow maps like Dust Bowl.

52
00:05:51,679 --> 00:05:55,679
Honestly, maps like Dust Bowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice

53
00:05:55,679 --> 00:05:56,679
the most seriously.

54
00:05:56,679 --> 00:06:01,679
They're old, restrictive maps where the balances age pretty badly, especially for

55
00:06:01,679 --> 00:06:02,679
red.

56
00:06:02,679 --> 00:06:07,559
The game could be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds, and the points can be captured absurdly quickly.

57
00:06:07,559 --> 00:06:10,919
And because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly

58
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:11,919
makes Scout amazing.

59
00:06:11,919 --> 00:06:15,739
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself, which fights

60
00:06:15,739 --> 00:06:18,799
you take, and whether you are actually where your team needs you.

61
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:22,319
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe.

62
00:06:22,319 --> 00:06:25,779
Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dust Bowl leaves you exposed to

63
00:06:25,779 --> 00:06:27,319
snipers and spam.

64
00:06:27,319 --> 00:06:31,459
Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point, and be prepared to back up once

65
00:06:31,459 --> 00:06:39,459
In this clip, I move around and help my team defend the choke, but I always end up returning to the same flank besides the point.

66
00:06:39,459 --> 00:06:44,459
It gives me cover, close range fights and an easy escape, while still allowing me to directly defend the point.

67
00:06:44,459 --> 00:06:50,459
It's also a really common place for blue to put a teleporter, so it's naturally somewhere Scout can find a lot of value.

68
00:06:50,459 --> 00:06:58,459
Target priority is also important. In general, if you see something like a direct hit soldier, there's a good chance he's using it because he wants to destroy your team's sentry nest.

69
00:06:58,459 --> 00:07:04,459
Scout can choose his fights, so you should use that freedom to focus the people who are most threatening to your team.

70
00:07:04,559 --> 00:07:08,459
You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps like Gold Rush and Dust Bowl.

71
00:07:08,699 --> 00:07:14,359
Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory and getting someone's attention and then immediately retreating.

72
00:07:14,839 --> 00:07:19,339
Impatient players will often overreact and chase you too far, which brings them directly into your team.

73
00:07:19,339 --> 00:07:24,139
That's what happens to this Pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then, and my soldier friend already

74
00:07:24,519 --> 00:07:28,099
was holding that position behind me and I knew that, so he deals with the Pyro straight away.

75
00:07:28,099 --> 00:07:33,099
I'm not just running away from the fight, I'm dragging the pirate into a fight that's much better for my team.

76
00:07:33,099 --> 00:07:54,659
And give Coldouts a scout because I already mentioned this but he cold scout for a reason and you often the first person who can see what Blue is doing so make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about It doesn need to be complicated you know it just that their combo is coming through the top left flank or a pyro is jetpacking towards you or someone is moving into your team space

77
00:07:54,759 --> 00:07:58,959
Even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalise.

78
00:07:58,959 --> 00:08:04,499
Now I know not everyone wants to treat casual like a scrim and constantly give out coal

79
00:08:04,499 --> 00:08:08,919
outs, but this 6's clip is the clearest way I can show you how powerful they are, and

80
00:08:08,919 --> 00:08:14,039
how powerful Scalper comes when his team can actually capitalise on the openings he creates.

81
00:08:14,039 --> 00:08:17,919
I don't create this opening by dropping the medic, I create it by forcing basically their

82
00:08:17,919 --> 00:08:20,579
entire team to turn around and focus on me.

83
00:08:20,579 --> 00:08:23,639
For those few seconds, none of them are looking at my team, which gives everyone else the

84
00:08:23,639 --> 00:08:27,619
opportunity to move forward and wipe out basically their entire team.

85
00:08:27,619 --> 00:08:29,979
But that's only powerful if my team knows what's happening.

86
00:08:29,979 --> 00:08:34,739
I can't expect them to capitalise on the entire enemy team looking at me unless I call it

87
00:08:34,739 --> 00:08:36,379
out and make them aware.

88
00:08:36,379 --> 00:08:40,939
Once I do, they know that the opening exists and can immediately take advantage of it.

89
00:08:40,939 --> 00:08:44,419
And this is what it looks like in full swing when your team capitalises on the opening

90
00:08:44,419 --> 00:08:45,419
Scout creates.

91
00:08:45,419 --> 00:08:49,319
Your value isn't always the player you kill, sometimes it's how much attention you pull

92
00:08:49,319 --> 00:08:53,759
away from everyone else, and this absolutely applies to casual as well.

93
00:08:53,759 --> 00:08:58,299
People just don't communicate it nearly enough, especially a scout.

